WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to the Federal
Modernization Workbook.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:05.549
This is Episode 2 of the Start series.

3
00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:10.925
Episode 1 asked leadership to approve a
four-week pre-flight, called Phase zero.

4
00:00:10.925 --> 00:00:14.681
This episode opens that box:
what has to be true on day one,

5
00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:18.028
what the team builds and
reads in those four weeks,

6
00:00:18.028 --> 00:00:22.437
and what the first gate, G zero,
checks before anyone plans a change.

7
00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:25.463
By the end, you'll know what
you're holding at week four.

8
00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.900
Let's start with the shape of the phase.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.438
Phase zero has one goal and one exit.

10
00:00:34.438 --> 00:00:36.787
The goal is to produce
the planning artifacts

11
00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:39.075
and stand up the platform
that will hold them.

12
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:42.978
The exit is gate G zero, Ready
to Plan: the platform works,

13
00:00:42.978 --> 00:00:47.575
the assessment is committed, and
no priority-one finding is open.

14
00:00:47.575 --> 00:00:48.738
Two cautions.

15
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:53.899
Four weeks is illustrative, so plan from
the milestones, not the week count.

16
00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.825
And nothing moves before the assessment
is done; a plan without one is a guess.

17
00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.130
So what has to be true on day one?

18
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.330
Three things have to
exist before week one.

19
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:12.606
First, two names: an executive sponsor,
who owns scope, funding and sequence,

20
00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:16.930
and a steward, who runs the
program and decides the first gate.

21
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.280
Second, a server to hold the work.

22
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.267
Third, a read-only account for the assessment,
with no administrator rights at all.

23
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.281
The names may already exist.

24
00:01:26.281 --> 00:01:31.605
Federal policy already required a zero
trust lead and an identity governance group,

25
00:01:31.605 --> 00:01:34.267
so build from those
people, not beside them.

26
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.230
Next, that server.

27
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.103
The platform host is one governed
server, with a standby for recovery,

28
00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:47.068
and it's where the program
writes everything down.

29
00:01:47.068 --> 00:01:49.553
What matters is one authoritative writer,

30
00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:52.393
so there's never a question
of which copy is right.

31
00:01:52.393 --> 00:01:55.343
The host reads the old
directory without changing it,

32
00:01:55.343 --> 00:01:59.031
and it writes to the cloud only
through plans someone has approved.

33
00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:02.342
It's protected like the most
sensitive identity systems.

34
00:02:02.342 --> 00:02:06.518
And it's a reference design built from
standard parts, not a product to buy.

35
00:02:06.518 --> 00:02:08.240
Now, the reading.

36
00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:15.378
The assessment reads the whole
estate and changes nothing.

37
00:02:15.378 --> 00:02:18.673
That's enforced three ways:
the account can't write,

38
00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:22.290
the code has no write commands,
and every call is logged.

39
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.990
It runs in two tiers.

40
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.124
A plain account reads what
default permissions expose,

41
00:02:27.124 --> 00:02:29.440
which is usually more than people expect.

42
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.552
Then a few narrow, read-only
additions fill the gaps it recorded,

43
00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:36.953
and nothing that exposes
secrets is ever requested.

44
00:02:36.953 --> 00:02:39.247
And tell security operations first:

45
00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:42.689
a full directory read looks
a lot like reconnaissance.

46
00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:44.450
So what does it inventory?

47
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.040
Here's what it reads: the directory's
structure, every kind of account,

48
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.291
every Group Policy Object, the network
services that lean on the directory,

49
00:02:56.291 --> 00:02:59.170
the certificate authorities
and their templates,

50
00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.913
and the applications that
bind to the directory.

51
00:03:01.913 --> 00:03:05.237
For any application that
might move away from its data,

52
00:03:05.237 --> 00:03:06.937
it measures how chatty it is.

53
00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:11.045
Out of this come risk findings,
like unsafe delegation or

54
00:03:11.045 --> 00:03:15.676
servers on Windows Server 2016,
and every one gets a disposition.

55
00:03:15.676 --> 00:03:19.050
But a list only helps if you
can prove where it came from.

56
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.729
Every assessment run carries its
own receipt: who ran it, when,

57
00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:30.030
against which forest and
which domain controller,

58
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.038
with which rights, and a fingerprint
of every file it produced.

59
00:03:34.038 --> 00:03:36.649
A run without that receipt is rejected.

60
00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:40.525
The receipt lands in the
repository, which this phase seeds.

61
00:03:40.525 --> 00:03:44.300
From here on, every artifact is
a reviewed change with a status,

62
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.000
and nothing becomes official
until the steward approves it.

63
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.495
The inventory itself is sensitive,
so it's stored encrypted,

64
00:03:51.495 --> 00:03:54.562
and the assessment workstation
is wiped afterward.

65
00:03:54.562 --> 00:03:57.300
Next, the program's own access.

66
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.862
The program practices what it asks of
everyone else, starting with its own access.

67
00:04:05.862 --> 00:04:10.412
The directory reader is an ordinary
account, never a domain administrator.

68
00:04:10.412 --> 00:04:14.956
The connections to cloud identity,
device management and server management

69
00:04:14.956 --> 00:04:19.500
each get their own service identity, with
only the permissions that job needs.

70
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.242
And the part of the platform that approves
a change runs under different credentials

71
00:04:24.242 --> 00:04:28.450
from the part that carries it out, so no
single account can both decide and act.

72
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:29.980
Then come the people.

73
00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:37.305
By week four, every finding
has a class and an owner.

74
00:04:37.305 --> 00:04:42.030
Critical findings block the gate: each
one is fixed, or formally accepted

75
00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:46.518
by the authorizing official, the only
person who can accept that risk.

76
00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:49.818
High findings get an owner and
a date before the next gate.

77
00:04:49.818 --> 00:04:51.242
The people are named too.

78
00:04:51.242 --> 00:04:54.407
The steward decides the gate,
with the sponsor as backup,

79
00:04:54.407 --> 00:04:57.917
and the workstream leads review
what was found in their areas.

80
00:04:57.917 --> 00:05:01.693
The steering committee advises; it
doesn't add a second signature.

81
00:05:01.693 --> 00:05:03.520
So what does the gate check?

82
00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:12.757
Gate G zero is a checklist, answered from
the repository, not from a status meeting.

83
00:05:12.757 --> 00:05:14.208
Is the platform working,

84
00:05:14.208 --> 00:05:18.270
with its service identities scoped
and its logs in central logging?

85
00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:22.570
Is the assessment committed, covering
every area, with its receipts?

86
00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:26.333
Is every finding classified,
with nothing critical left open?

87
00:05:26.333 --> 00:05:30.170
And is the decision itself recorded,
with the name of whoever made it?

88
00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.695
The answer can be go, no-go,
or go with conditions.

89
00:05:33.695 --> 00:05:38.460
A gate passed on a date without the
evidence just moves the failure later.

90
00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.910
At week four, leadership holds
facts instead of estimates.

91
00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.735
An inventory of the whole estate,
with proof of how it was read.

92
00:05:49.735 --> 00:05:53.385
A findings list where every
item has an owner and a date.

93
00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:56.835
A platform that records every
decision and who made it.

94
00:05:56.835 --> 00:05:59.010
And a gate decision, on the record.

95
00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:03.412
Right after the gate, the assessment
becomes eight modernization plans,

96
00:06:03.412 --> 00:06:08.035
generated from data rather than drawn up
by hand, and approved by the steward.

97
00:06:08.035 --> 00:06:10.906
That's where the foundation work begins,

98
00:06:10.906 --> 00:06:14.800
and it's the subject of Episode
3: Foundation to Gate G1.
